  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday Night through Wednesday)\nIssued at 1031 PM CST Wed Nov 18 2026\n\nThe synoptic pattern more or less remains the same through the \nend of the week, with well above average temperatures and breezy \nsouth winds persisting through Saturday. Pressure gradients will \nbe tightest Friday and Saturday ahead of an approaching cold \nfront, leading to afternoon wind gusts between 20 to 30 mph. While\nthese factors will tend to increase fire spread rates, especially\nNW of I- 44, increasing RHs should keep the concern limited \noverall.\n\nAn upper level wave and associated sfc low are forecast to move \neast across Oklahoma late Saturday into Sunday, alongside the \naforementioned cold front. This will provide increasing rain \nchances and low thunder potential, though instability appears weak\nat best. Total rainfall has generally trended up from previous \nforecast cycles, and while rain amounts still appear to be around \na quarter of an inch or less for most, scattered totals near a \nhalf inch or greater cannot be ruled out. Highest rain amounts are\nforecast north of I-40.\n\nGusty north winds and colder temperatures will fill in behind the\nfront Sunday. Near or below average temperatures will then \npersist through early next week within post-frontal high pressure.\nAnother system will approach the region Tuesday/Tuesday night and\nmay bring additional rain chances to E OK, but confidence is low.",
